{
  "dom_id": "step-q104046",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200385/assets/e54c40efd2e67f41ab726eaf3ef13c56aab8acb56222e8db3c53de47e6bcf547.png)\n\nThe graph $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Of the following, which best describes the function $y=f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,5]?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that $y={f}^{′}(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis (where we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$) twice, at the points $A,$ $B$ and $C$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200385/assets/76c04c26b2641990e4528fb3e89c4ef72c7a27c67e289c88cb1d6034baf67ef7.png)\n\nAn extremum of $y=f(x)$ occurs when the graph $y={f}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign. Hence, we have a relative minimum at $A,$  since the derivative goes from negative to positive, and a relative maximum at $B,$  since the derivative goes from positive to negative.\n\nNow, an inflection point may occur where the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is zero (the tangent line to $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is horizontal) or $y={f}^{″}(x)$ does not exist (a rapid change in the slope). These are the points $C$, $D,$ $E,$ and $F$ as shown on the graph below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200385/assets/1264de8104784e808891fbe44b176e0624e05fa1ea8058cf533c732016eca63d.png)\n\n \n\nWe obtain an inflection point if the slope of the tangent line to $y={f}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign. Thus, we have inflection points only at $C,D,$ and $E.$ Indeed, at points $C$ and $E$ the slope goes from positive to negative, while at $D$ the slope goes from negative to positive.\n\nTherefore, we get two relative extrema and three inflection points on $[-2,5]$ for $y=f(x).$",
